well russia's president vladimir putin  has played down the damage of economic  sanctions and claimed his country will  be stronger in the longer term questions  remain though is mr putin just putting  on a brave face and how much concern is  there within the kremlin about the  growing economic cost of his uh military  operation here  let's talk now to christopher steele a  former british intelligence officer who  was stationed in moscow for a number of  years thank you very much for  being uh with us christopher and you've  been  watching russia and watching putin  closer than most do you think this  moment was always going to come  hello mark thank you for having me on  the show  um i don't believe it was inevitable we  would get to this point  however i do believe that given  the perception that putin had of the  weakness and divisions in the west and  the likely western reaction to such an  adventure as this  that that is why we've got to this point  fundamentally  did you think ever think that he would  go  this far in pursuit of his goals  to be honest i didn't think he would  launch a full-scale invasion of ukraine  i thought it was highly likely that he  would launch an operation to take  lugansk and donaire's obelisks  and possibly reincorporate them into  russia  but clearly logistically and  economically and in terms of manpower  an operation on this scale is really  beyond russia and he's overreached this  time in my opinion  do you think he's miscalculated do you  miscalculated militarily in terms of the  ukrainian resistance and miscalculated  in you know terms of the  the west imposing tougher sanctions than  maybe expected  i think there are several gross  miscalculations here by putin and one of  the problems i think is when you run an  authoritarian regime like he does  and you don't welcome criticism or  debates within the leadership  then you end up with bad advice and  hearing the things you want to hear from  your advisors and i think the obvious  things here that have gone badly wrong  for the russians the performance of  their own army is not great by any  standards it wasn't either in georgia or  chechnya before that so it shouldn't  necessarily come as a surprise to us but  it's certainly not what putin was  expecting  in addition to that you've got the  brave ukrainian resistance particularly  president zielinski who putin didn't  take seriously  and he's had a bite back there  and then you've got as you say the  degree to which um western governments  have reacted this time overdue in my  opinion but nevertheless strongly and in  a united way  which has come as a surprise i'm sure to  mr putin and his advisors  what do you make of what boris johnson  has been saying to sky news uh this  evening that um  there may be  an issue here where he's paving the way  for the use of  chemical weapons do you think he would  do that  i certainly think if you look at what's  happened in syria  and you look at what's happened in  chechnya  and of course what happened on our own  streets in salisbury um i wouldn't rule  it out at all and i think that  as the russian army becomes bogged down  and more desperate and clearly not  realizing its objectives militarily you  are likely to see  more indiscriminate killing  and bombardment and possibly the use of  a weapon as you describe it  and what do you think of the way nato is  dealing with this do you think that nato  should be  you know  publicly setting down setting out its  red lines  i think nato has probably set down some  of the red lines although clearly there  are war crimes going on in ukraine as we  speak and those need to be brought to  justice in due course in the future  i think that it's very important that  militarily russia fails in this  operation and i think it will only fail  if we continue to supply  handheld weapons anti-tank weapons  anti-aircraft weapons  on a very large scale and indeed provide  economic support to the ukrainian  government and its forces  do you think this is a different  vladimir putin to  the leader that you saw  um a few years ago  fundamentally not  i think putin's family and his  background was forged by the siege of  leningrad in the second world war where  one million people died  and i don't think that the culture that  he comes out of allows him to escape  from a kind of fear of encirclement  and a zero-sum view of world politics  and world affairs  and also i would argue a lack of  understanding of economy because economy  in the end is what will stop russia on  its tracks and what will probably lead  to the fall of this regime sooner rather  than later  because of the weakness of russia  economically russia has an economy which  is a tenth the size of just the eu let  alone the us  as well and that will in the end tell i  believe  what are the chances do you think  christopher of someone in the kremlin  someone who gets close to vladimir putin  um  taking him out putting an end to this is  that at all a possibility  i certainly think it's a possibility  it's happened in russia before obviously  it happened to nikita khrushchev  after his failure at the cuban missile  crisis  i think it's certainly a possibility i  suspect that putin is very well  protected and paranoid about the people  he has physically around him but i still  suspect that this is we've seen the high  water mark of putinism and that this  overreached adventure and despicable  operation in ukraine will eventually  lead to his downfall and that of the  regime  and if it's not someone in the kremlin  what is the possibility of  you know the people  turning against him that the protests  just gather in pace so much so that um  it becomes very difficult for putin to  carry on i assume that you know being a  kind of dictatorship that's not going to  happen  i  don't think it will be decisive but i  think it will probably be a factor as  the economy is squeezed the sanctions  start to bite which they certainly will  and as people's living standards start  to fall there will be increased protests  on the streets of moscow and petersburg  and other cities and i think that will  play in to probably an elite attempt to  remove putin in due course  and just finally  30 seconds or so how do you see this  playing out  does  does putin come out you know from this  does he survive this do you think  i think putin can't be resurrected as it  were in the international community i  don't see him  surviving this in the long term i think  we've gone over a watershed here  and my view is however that in the short  term we're going to see probably  increased brutality from russian forces  and atrocities in ukraine sadly but in  the longer term the economy and the  sanctions and his isolation both  cultural and economic in the world will  tell and will lead to a change of regime  in russia i certainly hope so  christopher thank you very much indeed
 

